import{c as t,Q as o,j as a,m as i}from"./chunks/framework.BXc4yaFE.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"The 4 loops that quietly killed prompt engineering","description":"","frontmatter":{"url":"https://x.com/dunik_7/status/2069079047510864322","title":"The 4 loops that quietly killed prompt engineering","author":"dunik_7","archived":"2026-06-27T00:00:00.000Z","handle":"@dunik_7","published":"2026-06-22T00:00:00.000Z","stats":"314 likes · 42 retweets · 1018 bookmarks · 330674 views","tags":["loop-engineering","verification-loop","hill-climbing","self-improvement","langchain"],"type":"twitter-article"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-22-@dunik_7-864322.md","filePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-22-@dunik_7-864322.md","lastUpdated":1785909234000}'),n={name:"research/tweets/2026-06-22-@dunik_7-864322.md"};function r(l,e,s,h,p,d){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="the-4-loops-that-quietly-killed-prompt-engineering-—-dunik-7" tabindex="-1">The 4 loops that quietly killed prompt engineering — @dunik_7 <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-4-loops-that-quietly-killed-prompt-engineering-—-dunik-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The 4 loops that quietly killed prompt engineering — @dunik_7&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>來源</strong>：<a href="https://x.com/dunik_7/status/2069079047510864322" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://x.com/dunik_7/status/2069079047510864322</a><strong>作者</strong>：dunik_7（@dunik_7） <strong>發布日期</strong>：2026-06-22 <strong>收錄日期</strong>：2026-06-27 <strong>類型</strong>：Twitter Article <strong>統計</strong>：314 likes · 42 retweets · 1018 bookmarks · 330674 views</p><hr><h2 id="全文" tabindex="-1">全文 <a class="header-anchor" href="#全文" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;全文&quot;">​</a></h2><p>1.01^365 = 37.8. LangChain just shipped the 4-loop playbook that gets you there while you sleep. most people are still hand-typing prompts into one agent, one request at a time. stack all four and your agents grade, fix, and rewrite themselves. you wake up to a better one than you went to bed with. I parked on rung 2 for the better part of a year. So did almost everyone I know building this stuff. Loop engineering hit 6.5M views the same week LangChain put out the playbook, and I don&#39;t think a single person noticed they were the same thing. The model hasn&#39;t been the bottleneck for months. What&#39;s left is the harness around it, and a harness is really just loops inside loops. The whole pitch comes down to one swap: you stop being the thing that prompts the agent, and you go build the thing that prompts the agent for you. Four rungs. Most people quietly fall off at the second one.</p><p>[圖 1]</p><hr><h1 id="loop-1-the-agent-loop" tabindex="-1">Loop 1 - the Agent loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#loop-1-the-agent-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loop 1 - the Agent loop&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Model calls a tool, reads what comes back, calls another, keeps going until the task is done. You already have this one.</p><p>[圖 2]</p><p>/ give it context / give it tools / let it run until &quot;done&quot; LangChain primitive: <code>create_agent</code>. This is the floor, not the ceiling. Stop here and what you&#39;ve actually got is a fancier autocomplete.</p><p>[圖 3]</p><hr><h1 id="loop-2-the-verification-loop" tabindex="-1">Loop 2 - the Verification loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#loop-2-the-verification-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loop 2 - the Verification loop&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The agent finishes, and instead of you eyeballing the output, a grader scores it against a rubric. If it&#39;s under the bar the feedback goes straight back in and it tries again. No human standing there clicking retry.</p><p>[圖 4]</p><p>/ deterministic checks for the boring stuff (links resolve, CI passes, scope matches the ask) / LLM-as-judge for the fuzzy stuff (did it actually answer the question) LangChain primitive: <code>RubricMiddleware</code>. It runs maybe 2-3x the tokens per task, yeah. But you&#39;re spending cents so the agent never hands a customer a wrong answer, and one wrong answer in prod costs you more than a thousand retries ever will. This is where 90% of people stop. It&#39;s also, annoyingly, exactly where the money was the whole time.</p><p>[圖 5]</p><hr><h1 id="loop-3-the-event-driven-loop" tabindex="-1">Loop 3 - the Event-driven loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#loop-3-the-event-driven-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loop 3 - the Event-driven loop&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Here&#39;s where it stops waiting for you to open a terminal. A message in <code>#docs-plz</code> kicks it off. A webhook kicks it off. A 3am cron job I half-forgot I set kicks it off. Nobody invokes it. It just runs, at scale, inside the tools you&#39;re already in all day.</p><p>[圖 6]</p><p>/ no human invocation / lives where you already work LangChain primitive: LangSmith Deployment with cron / webhooks, or Fleet channels. At that point it isn&#39;t an app you go visit anymore. It&#39;s a coworker who&#39;s always on and never files an invoice.</p><p>[圖 7]</p><hr><h1 id="loop-4-the-hill-climbing-loop" tabindex="-1">Loop 4 - the Hill-climbing loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#loop-4-the-hill-climbing-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loop 4 - the Hill-climbing loop&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This is the one that took me a while to actually believe. Every run leaves a trace. Those traces feed an analysis agent that reads them, spots the failures that keep happening, and rewrites the prompt and tool config of Loop 1.</p><p>[圖 8]</p><p>So the return arrow doesn&#39;t go back to the top. It reaches <em>inside</em> and edits the agent itself. / the thing notices where it keeps screwing up / then it patches its own setup / you wake up to a better agent than the one you shut the laptop on LangChain primitive: LangSmith Engine.</p><p>[圖 9]</p><hr><h1 id="the-part-the-langchain-ad-accidentally-got-right" tabindex="-1">The part the LangChain ad accidentally got right <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-part-the-langchain-ad-accidentally-got-right" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The part the LangChain ad accidentally got right&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The funny thing is it&#39;s a LangSmith ad, and it&#39;s still completely right. Loops 1 and 2 are where everyone&#39;s elbowing each other. Better prompts, better models, better graders. Packed. Loops 3 and 4 are basically empty. That&#39;s the whole edge, sitting there. The companies that win next year won&#39;t be the ones with the best model. Everyone rents the same model anyway, same weights, same price. They&#39;ll be the ones whose agent got 1% better every single night for a year, on its own, while the competition was still typing prompts by hand. 37x, if you trust the math. Prompt engineering had a good run. It got replaced by the boring skill of building the loop that prompts for you. And the last loop? Nobody had to prompt it. It prompted itself.</p><p>[圖 10]</p><hr><h2 id="研究摘要" tabindex="-1">研究摘要 <a class="header-anchor" href="#研究摘要" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;研究摘要&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>TL;DR</strong>：基於 LangChain 4-Loop 路線圖提出漸進式 Loop 工程框架，最關鍵的 Loop 4（Hill-climbing）——每次執行留 trace，分析 agent 讀取失敗模式後自動改寫 Loop 1 的 prompt 和工具配置，實現「夜間自我進化」。</p><h3 id="核心論點" tabindex="-1">核心論點 <a class="header-anchor" href="#核心論點" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;核心論點&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>4-Loop 分類</strong>：Agent Loop（基礎）→ Verification Loop（rubric 評分 + 自動重試）→ Event-driven Loop（webhook/cron 無人觸發）→ Hill-climbing Loop（trace 分析 → 自動改寫 prompt/config）</li><li><strong>90% 人止步 Loop 2</strong>（Verification Loop）—— 「That&#39;s also, annoyingly, exactly where the money was the whole time」</li><li><strong>Hill-climbing Loop 核心原理</strong>：<code>return arrow reaches inside and edits the agent itself</code>——trace → 失敗模式分析 → 改寫 Loop 1 的 prompt 和工具配置，不是重新執行，是改寫執行環境</li><li><strong>競爭優勢框架</strong>：1.01^365 = 37.8；每晚 1% 改進 × 365 天 = 37x，競爭護城河不在模型（大家租一樣的），在 loop 自我改進的速度</li><li><strong>Loops 3+4 是空地</strong>：大家都在 Loop 1-2 競爭，Loop 3 和 4 「basically empty」</li></ol><h3 id="關鍵概念" tabindex="-1">關鍵概念 <a class="header-anchor" href="#關鍵概念" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;關鍵概念&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>LangChain primitives 對應</strong>：create_agent / RubricMiddleware / LangSmith Deployment + Fleet channels / LangSmith Engine</li><li><strong>Verification 兩類檢查</strong>：deterministic（links/CI/scope）+ LLM-as-judge（模糊品質）</li><li><strong>Hill-climbing 三步</strong>：notice where it keeps screwing up → patch its own setup → wake up to a better agent</li></ul><h3 id="批判性觀察" tabindex="-1">批判性觀察 <a class="header-anchor" href="#批判性觀察" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;批判性觀察&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li>Hill-climbing Loop 直接對應 workspace core.md RECORD 章節的「自我演化迴圈」定義（失敗→反思→下次注入），但文章的 trace-based 機制（每次執行留 trace → 分析 agent 讀取 → 改寫 prompt）比 core.md 更具體，提供了工程實作路徑</li><li>Loop 2 Verification 的「deterministic + LLM-as-judge 雙層」結構與 akshay_pachaar 文章的「maker-checker 分離」完全一致，互為佐證</li><li>文章偏向 LangSmith 產品推廣（RubricMiddleware/LangSmith Engine），通用實作細節不足；但概念框架（4 分類）是 framework-agnostic 的</li></ol><hr><h2 id="評分" tabindex="-1">評分 <a class="header-anchor" href="#評分" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;評分&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>維度</th><th>分數</th><th>說明</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Workspace 可行動性 (30%)</td><td>7/10</td><td>Hill-climbing = core.md 自我演化迴圈的具體化；4-loop 分類補充 loop 層次命名</td></tr><tr><td>B. 創新性 (20%)</td><td>6/10</td><td>Hill-climbing loop 概念（return arrow reaches inside）是最具原創性的部分；其他分類較常見</td></tr><tr><td>C. 證據品質 (20%)</td><td>5/10</td><td>6.5M 瀏覽量引用、LangChain 產品名（有公信力），但缺具體數據；Twitter Article 上限 6</td></tr><tr><td>D. 技術深度 (15%)</td><td>6/10</td><td>LangChain primitives 名稱有具體指向，但無代碼/流程細節</td></tr><tr><td>E. 泛化性 (15%)</td><td>8/10</td><td>4-loop 框架概念 framework-agnostic；37x 複利論點普遍適用</td></tr><tr><td><strong>加權總分</strong></td><td><strong>6.4/10</strong></td><td>7×0.3+6×0.2+5×0.2+6×0.15+8×0.15 = 2.1+1.2+1.0+0.9+1.2</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>整合決策</strong>：Rule <strong>理由</strong>：Hill-climbing Loop（trace → 失敗模式分析 → 改寫 prompt/config）直接補充 core.md RECORD §自我演化迴圈的工程化路徑；4-loop 分類提供層次命名框架</p>',47)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{c as __pageData,u as default};
